\end_inset

Money lotteries and risk aversion
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
6.4.1
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Expected utility theory
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Primitives of the model:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Set of prices 
\begin_inset Formula $Z\equiv\mathbb{R}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Alternative set 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta(Z)$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition*

\series bold
(117).

\series default
 
\emph on
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

 be a preference on 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta(\mathbb{R})$
\end_inset

.
 Then, 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

 is 
\series bold
money monotone
\series default
 iff 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\ \{z,z^{\prime}\}\subseteq\mathbb{R}:z\geq z^{\prime}\implies\delta_{z}\succsim\delta_{z^{\prime}}$
\end_inset

; it is 
\series bold
strictly money monotone
\series default
 iff 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\ \{z,z^{\prime}\}\subseteq\mathbb{R}:z>z^{\prime}\implies\delta_{z}\succ\delta_{z^{\prime}}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Note*
\begin_inset Formula $z^{\prime}\geq z\implies\delta_{z^{\prime}}\succsim\delta_{z}\implies U(\delta_{z^{\prime}})\geq U(\delta_{z^{\prime}})\implies u(z^{\prime})\geq u(z)$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Theorem*

\series bold
\shape up
(23).

\series default
 
\emph on
A preference on 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

 on 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta(\mathbb{R})$
\end_inset

 satisfies A1, A2*, and A3* if and only if there is an index 
\begin_inset Formula $u:\mathbb{R}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}$
\end_inset

 such that for each 
\begin_inset Formula $\{F,G\}\subseteq\Delta(\mathbb{R})$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $F\succsim G$
\end_inset

 if and only if
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
E_{F}u\geq E_{G}u
\]

\end_inset

The index 
\begin_inset Formula $u$
\end_inset

 is unique up to a positive affine linear transformation.
 Moreover, if 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

 is money monotone, then 
\begin_inset Formula $u$
\end_inset

 is non-decreasing; and if 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

 is strictly money monotone, then 
\begin_inset Formula $u$
\end_inset

 is increasing.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
This proof is analogous to the proof of Theorem 21.
 However, the proof is very technical, and it was not covered during class.
 Therefore, it will not be presented in these notes.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Remark*
We assume that the index 
\begin_inset Formula $u(\cdot)$
\end_inset

 is a continuous function.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
6.4.2
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Risk Aversion
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The concept of risk aversion provides one of the central analytical techniques
 of economic analysis.
 We begin our discussion of risk aversion with a simple example and a general
 definition that does not presume an expected utility formulation.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Consider the lottery 
\begin_inset Formula $F$
\end_inset

 that assigns probability 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{1}{2}$
\end_inset

 to the outcomes 
\begin_inset Formula $\$1$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $-\$1$
\end_inset

.
 Consider an agent who chooses between 
\begin_inset Formula $F$
\end_inset

 and the lottery that places probability one in 
\begin_inset Formula $\$0$
\end_inset

, i.e., 
\begin_inset Formula $\delta_{0}$
\end_inset

.
 What would an agent who is 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

averse to risk
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 choose? Lottery 
\begin_inset Formula $F$
\end_inset

 offers only risk compared to 
\begin_inset Formula $\delta_{0}$
\end_inset

.
 Thus, a risk averse agent would prefer 
\begin_inset Formula $\delta_{0}$
\end_inset

 to 
\begin_inset Formula $F$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition*

\series bold
(118).

\series default
 
\emph on
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

 be a preference on 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta(\mathbb{R})$
\end_inset

.
 Then, 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

 is
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Enumerate

\series bold
\emph on
risk averse
\series default
 iff 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\ F\in\Delta(\mathbb{R}):\delta_{\mu_{F}}\succsim F$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate

\series bold
\emph on
strictly risk averse
\series default
 iff 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\ F\in\Delta(\mathbb{R}):\delta_{\mu_{F}}\neq F\implies\delta_{\mu_{F}}\succ F$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate

\series bold
\emph on
risk neutral
\series default
 iff 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\ F\in\Delta(\mathbb{R}):\delta_{\mu_{F}}\sim F$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Subsection*
6.4.3
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Index characterization of risk aversion
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proposition*

\series bold
\shape up
(59).

\series default
 
\emph on
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

 be an EU maximizer with associated index 
\begin_inset Formula $u$
\end_inset

.
 Then, 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

 is risk averse if and only if 
\begin_inset Formula $u$
\end_inset

 is concave.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

 be an EU maximizaer with associated index 
\begin_inset Formula $u$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula $(\Rightarrow)$
\end_inset

 Assume 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

 is risk averse.
 We prove that 
\begin_inset Formula $u$
\end_inset

 is concave.
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $\{x,y\}\subseteq\mathbb{R}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha\in(0,1)$
\end_inset

.
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $F$
\end_inset

 be the lottery that assigns probability 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha$
\end_inset

 to 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $(1-\alpha)$
\end_inset

 to 
\begin_inset Formula $y$
\end_inset

.
 Since 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

 is risk averse, then
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\delta_{\alpha x+(1-\alpha)y}\succsim F
\]

\end_inset

Thus,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
U(\delta_{\alpha x+(1-\alpha)y})=u(\alpha+(1-\alpha)y)\geq\alpha u(x)+(1-\alpha)u(y)=U(F)
\]

\end_inset

which proves that 
\begin_inset Formula $u$
\end_inset

 is concave.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
\begin_inset Formula $(\Leftarrow)$
\end_inset

 Assume that 
\begin_inset Formula $u$
\end_inset

 is concave.
 We prove that 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

 is risk averse.
 Take 
\begin_inset Formula $F\in\Delta(\mathbb{R})$
\end_inset

.
 We claim that 
\begin_inset Formula $\delta_{\mu_{F}}\succsim F$
\end_inset

.
 Consider the tangent to 
\begin_inset Formula $u$
\end_inset

 at 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu_{F}$
\end_inset

.
 The equation of this line is, for each 
\begin_inset Formula $t\in\mathbb{R}$
\end_inset

,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
y(t)=u(\mu_{F})+u^{\prime}(\mu_{F})(t-\mu_{F})
\]

\end_inset

Since 
\begin_inset Formula $u$
\end_inset

 is concave, then for each 
\begin_inset Formula $t\in\mathbb{R}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $y(t)\geq u(t)$
\end_inset

.
 Thus,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
{\displaystyle \int}y(t)dF(t)\geq{\displaystyle \int}u(t)dF(t)
\]

\end_inset

which is
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
{\displaystyle \int}\left[u(\mu_{F})+u^{\prime}(\mu_{F})(t-\mu_{F})\right]dF(t) & \geq & {\displaystyle \int}u(t)dF(t)\\
u(\mu_{F}){\displaystyle \int}dF(t)+u^{\prime}(\mu_{F}){\displaystyle \int}tdF(t)-u^{\prime}(\mu_{F})\mu_{F}{\displaystyle \int}dF(t) & \geq & {\displaystyle \int}u(t)dF(t)
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

Since 
\begin_inset Formula $\int dF=1$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\int tdF(t)=\mu_{F}$
\end_inset

, then
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
u\left(\int tdF(t)\right)\geq{\displaystyle \int}u(t)dF(t)
\]

\end_inset

where the above equation is known as 
\emph on
Jensen's Inequality
\emph default
, and it holds for all 
\begin_inset Formula $F(\cdot)$
\end_inset

.
 It is the defining property of a concave function.
 Thus, 
\begin_inset Formula $\delta_{\mu_{F}}\succsim F$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Hence, in the context of expected utility theory, we see that risk aversion
 is equivalent to the concavity of 
\begin_inset Formula $u(\cdot)$
\end_inset

, and that strict risk aversion is equivalent to the strict concavity of
 
\begin_inset Formula $u(\cdot)$
\end_inset

.
 Strict concavity means that the marginal utility of money is decreasing.
 Hence, at any level of wealth 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

, the utility gain from an extra dollar is small than (the absolute value
 of) the utility loss of having a dollar less.
 It follows that a risk of gaining or losing a dollar with even proability
 is not worth taking.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

For a risk-neutral expected utility maximizer, Jensen's inequality must
 hold with 
\emph on
equality
\emph default
 for all 
\begin_inset Formula $F(\cdot)$
\end_inset

.
 Hence, the decision maker is risk neutral if and only if the index function
 
\begin_inset Formula $u(\cdot)$
\end_inset

 is linear.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
6.4.4
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Certainty equivalent and probability premium characterization of risk aversion
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition*

\series bold
(119).

\series default
 
\emph on
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

 be an EU maximizer with associated index 
\begin_inset Formula $u$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $F\in\Delta(\mathbb{R})$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Enumerate

\emph on
The 
\series bold
certainty equivalent of 
\begin_inset Formula $F$
\end_inset

 with respect to 
\begin_inset Formula $u$
\end_inset


\series default
 is the amount of money 
\begin_inset Formula $c(F,u)$
\end_inset

 such that
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\delta_{c(F,u)}\sim F
\]

\end_inset

Equivalently
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
u(c(F,u))={\displaystyle \int}u\ dF
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate

\emph on
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $x\in\mathbb{R}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\epsilon>0$
\end_inset

.
 The 
\series bold
probability premium at 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 with respect to 
\begin_inset Formula $\epsilon$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $u$
\end_inset


\series default
 is the premium 
\begin_inset Formula $\pi(x,\epsilon,u)$
\end_inset

 such that if 
\begin_inset Formula $F$
\end_inset

 is the lottery that assigns probability 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{1}{2}-\pi(x,\epsilon,u)$
\end_inset

 to 
\begin_inset Formula $x-\epsilon$
\end_inset

 and probability 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{1}{2}+\pi(x,\epsilon,u)$
\end_inset

 to 
\begin_inset Formula $x+\epsilon$
\end_inset

,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\delta_{x}\sim F
\]

\end_inset

Equivalently
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
U(x)=\left(\frac{1}{2}-\pi(x,\epsilon,u)\right)u(x-\epsilon)+\left(\frac{1}{2}+\pi(x,\epsilon,u)\right)u(x+\epsilon)
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Remark*
Note that 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu_{F}\geq c(F,u)$
\end_inset

.
 Also, 
\begin_inset Formula $\delta_{x}\sim F^{\alpha};\ \mbox{\alpha\geq\frac{1}{2};\ }\pi(x,\epsilon,u)=\alpha-\frac{1}{2}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Proposition*

\series bold
\shape up
(60).

\series default
 
\emph on
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

 be an EU maximizer with associated index 
\begin_inset Formula $u$
\end_inset

.
 Then, the following conditions are equivalent:
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Enumerate
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

 is risk averse.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
For each 
\begin_inset Formula $F\in\Delta(\mathbb{R})$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $c(F,u)\leq\mu_{F}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
For each 
\begin_inset Formula $x\in\mathbb{R}$
\end_inset

 and each 
\begin_inset Formula $\epsilon>0$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $\pi(x,\epsilon,u)\geq0$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
u is concave.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Proof
Will prove.
 Check page 187 of Mas-Colell.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
6.5
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Comparisons of risk aversion across agents
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Now that we know what it means to be risk averse, we can try to measure
 the extent of risk aversion.
 In addition, we can compare risk aversion across individuals.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition*

\series bold
(120).

\series default
 
\emph on
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim^{1}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim^{2}$
\end_inset

 be two risk averse EU maximizers.
 Then 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim^{1}$
\end_inset

 is more risk averse than 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim^{2}$
\end_inset

 iff 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\ F\in\Delta(Z),\ \forall\ c\in\mathbb{R}:$
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\left[F\succsim^{1}\delta_{c}\implies F\succsim^{2}\delta_{c}\right]
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Suppose that 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim^{1}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim^{2}$
\end_inset

 are two risk averse EU maximizers and that 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim^{1}$
\end_inset

 is more risk averse than 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim^{2}$
\end_inset

.
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{1}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{2}$
\end_inset

 be their respective utility indices.
 As we saw in the previous section, an agent is risk averse when his/her
 associated utility index is concave.
 As we will see, 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim^{1}$
\end_inset

 is more risk averse than 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim^{2}$
\end_inset

 whenever 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{1}$
\end_inset

 is 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

more concave
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 than 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{2}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The issue then is how to measure concavity of a function.
 We know that if the second derivative of a funciton is negative, then the
 function is concave.
 However, the comparison of second derivatives across functions does not
 tell us which function is more concave.
 Think for instance of 
\begin_inset Formula $u$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $2u$
\end_inset

.
 Clearly, these two functions represent the same agent.
 However, the absolute value of the second derivative of 
\begin_inset Formula $2u$
\end_inset

 is always twice that of 
\begin_inset Formula $u$
\end_inset

.
 This happens because the first derivative of 
\begin_inset Formula $2u$
\end_inset

 is always twice the first derivative of 
\begin_inset Formula $u$
\end_inset

.
 Thus, since the functions represent the same agent, the derivative of 
\begin_inset Formula $2u$
\end_inset

 has to decrease twice as fast as the derivative of 
\begin_inset Formula $u$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

This is why in order to learn 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

how concave a funciton 
\begin_inset Formula $u$
\end_inset

 is at 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

,
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 one has to normalize the first derivative of 
\begin_inset Formula $u$
\end_inset

 at 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

.
 Consider the function that assigns to each 
\begin_inset Formula $t\in\mathbb{R}$
\end_inset

 the value
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\frac{1}{u^{\prime}(x)}u(t)
\]

\end_inset

This function's derivative at 
\begin_inset Formula $t=x$
\end_inset

 is 1.
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\begin{array}{cccc}
\Phi^{u}: & \mathbb{R} & \rightarrow & \mathbb{R}\\
 & t & \mapsto & \frac{1}{u^{\prime}(x)}u(t)
\end{array}
\]

\end_inset

So, we have
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
\frac{d\Phi^{u}}{dt}(t) & = & \frac{u^{\prime}(t)}{u^{\prime}(x)}\\
\frac{d\Phi^{u}}{dt}(x) & = & 1
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

Thus, its second derivative evaluated at 
\begin_inset Formula $t=x$
\end_inset

, i.e.,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\frac{d^{2}\Phi^{u}}{dt^{2}}=\frac{1}{u^{\prime}(x)}u^{\prime\prime}(x)
\]

\end_inset

says how concave the funciton is at 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

, and can be compared to the second derivative of another function whose
 first derivative at 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 is also 1.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

We want to define an index that tells us how concave the function is.
 Since the second derivative of a concave function is non-positive, then
 we define the 
\series bold
coefficient of absolute risk aversion of 
\begin_inset Formula $u$
\end_inset

 at 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset


\series default
 as
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
r_{A}^{u}(x)\equiv-\frac{u^{\prime\prime}(x)}{u^{\prime}(x)}\geq0
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Note*
Higher values of 
\begin_inset Formula $r_{A}^{u}(x)$
\end_inset

 means 
\begin_inset Formula $u$
\end_inset

 is more concave.
 Also, the more risk averse the agent, the quicker the function goes down
 from the normalized slope.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Theorem*

\series bold
\shape up
(24).
 
\series default
\emph on
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim^{1}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim^{2}$
\end_inset

 be two risk averse EU maximizers with associated indices 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{1}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{2}$
\end_inset

, respectively.
 The following are equivalent.
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Enumerate
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim^{1}$
\end_inset

 is more risk averse than 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim^{2}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
For each 
\begin_inset Formula $F\in\Delta(\mathbb{R})$
\end_inset

 and each 
\begin_inset Formula $c\in\mathbb{R}$
\end_inset

,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
{\displaystyle \int}u_{1}dF\geq u_{1}(c)\implies\int u_{2}dF\geq u_{2}(c)
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
\begin_inset Formula $u_{1}$
\end_inset

 is more concave than 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{2}$
\end_inset

, i.e., there is an increasing and concave function 
\begin_inset Formula $\psi$
\end_inset

 such that 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{1}=\psi\circ u_{2}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
If 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{1}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{2}$
\end_inset

 are twice differentiable, then for each 
\begin_inset Formula $x\in\mathbb{R}$
\end_inset

,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
r_{A}^{u_{1}}(x)\geq r_{A}^{u_{2}}(x)
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
For each 
\begin_inset Formula $F\in\Delta(\mathbb{R})$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $c(F,u_{1})\leq c(F,u_{2})$
\end_inset


\end_layout

