\begin_layout Section*
6.6
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Comparisons of risk aversion across different levels of wealth
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Let us consider a risk EU maximizer with associated utility index 
\begin_inset Formula $u$
\end_inset

.
 We are interested in studying when does the agent become less risk averse
 as he/she becomes wealthier.
 The answer depends on the nature of the risk the agent faces
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
6.6.1
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Decreasing risk aversion to additive risk
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

First, we consider additive risks.
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $w\in\mathbb{R}$
\end_inset

 be the agent's initial wealth.
 Consider a random variable 
\begin_inset Formula $z$
\end_inset

 with distribution 
\begin_inset Formula $F$
\end_inset

.
 We interpret the random variable as an additive lottery that will realize
 an additive gain for the agent.
 Then, the final distribution of wealth of the agent is the random variable
 
\begin_inset Formula $w+z$
\end_inset

 where 
\begin_inset Formula $z$
\end_inset

 is distributed 
\begin_inset Formula $F$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The expected utility of 
\begin_inset Formula $w+F$
\end_inset

 is given by
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
U(w+F)\equiv{\displaystyle \int}u(w+t)\ dF(t)={\displaystyle \int}u(w+\cdot)\ dF
\]

\end_inset

That is, the expected utility of 
\begin_inset Formula $w+F$
\end_inset

 is also the expected utility of the random variable distributed 
\begin_inset Formula $F$
\end_inset

 for the agent with utility function 
\begin_inset Formula $u(w+\cdot)$
\end_inset

, which is the horizontal translation of 
\begin_inset Formula $u$
\end_inset

 to the left by 
\begin_inset Formula $w$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

We are interested in characterizing the conditions under which the agent
 more easily accepts an additive lottery when his/her wealth increases.
 That is, when is it true that if the agent prefers the addtive lottery
 
\begin_inset Formula $F$
\end_inset

 to the certain change in wealth 
\begin_inset Formula $c$
\end_inset

 at a level of wealth 
\begin_inset Formula $w$
\end_inset

, then he/she will prefer 
\begin_inset Formula $F$
\end_inset

 to the certain change in wealth 
\begin_inset Formula $c$
\end_inset

 at any level of wealth higher than 
\begin_inset Formula $w$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition*

\series bold
(121).

\series default
 
\emph on
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

 be a risk averse EU maximizer.
 Then 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

 exhibits 
\series bold
decreasing relative aversion to additive risks
\series default
 iff 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\ w_{2}>w_{1},\ \forall\ F\in\Delta(Z),\ \forall\ c\in\mathbb{R}:$
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\left[w_{1}+F\succsim\delta_{w_{1}+c}\implies w_{2}+F\succsim\delta_{w_{2}+c}\right]
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Theorem*

\series bold
\shape up
(25).

\series default
\emph on
 The following are equivalent.
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Enumerate
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

 exhibits decreasing risk aversion to additive risks.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
For each 
\begin_inset Formula $w_{2}>w_{1}$
\end_inset

, each 
\begin_inset Formula $F\in\Delta(\mathbb{R})$
\end_inset

, and each 
\begin_inset Formula $c\in\mathbb{R}$
\end_inset

,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
{\displaystyle \int}u(w_{1}+t)dF(t)\geq u(w_{1}+c)\implies{\displaystyle \int}u(w_{2}+t)dF(t)\geq u(w_{2}+c)
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
The EU preference associated with index 
\begin_inset Formula $u(w_{1}+\cdot)$
\end_inset

 is more risk averse than the preference associated with index 
\begin_inset Formula $u(w_{2}+\cdot)$
\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
For each 
\begin_inset Formula $w_{2}>w_{1}$
\end_inset

, the function 
\begin_inset Formula $u(w_{1}+\cdot)$
\end_inset

 is more concave than 
\begin_inset Formula $u(w_{2}+\cdot)$
\end_inset

.
 That is, there is an increasing and concave function 
\begin_inset Formula $\psi$
\end_inset

 such that for each 
\begin_inset Formula $t\in\mathbb{R}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $u(w_{1}+t)=\psi(u(w_{2}+t))$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
If 
\begin_inset Formula $u$
\end_inset

 is twice differentiable, the coefficient of absolute risk aversion for
 
\begin_inset Formula $u$
\end_inset

 is a non-increasing function, that is, if 
\begin_inset Formula $x>y$
\end_inset

, then
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
r_{A}^{u}(y)\geq r_{A}^{u}(x)
\]

\end_inset

To elaborate, if 
\begin_inset Formula $w_{2}>w_{1}$
\end_inset

, then
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
r_{A}^{u(w_{1}+\cdot)}(x)\geq r_{A}^{u(w_{2}+\cdot)}(x)
\]

\end_inset

where
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\begin{array}{cccc}
u(w+\cdot): & \mathbb{R} & \rightarrow & \mathbb{R}\\
 & x & \mapsto & u(w+\cdot)\equiv u(w+x)
\end{array}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
For each 
\begin_inset Formula $w_{2}>w_{1}$
\end_inset

 and each 
\begin_inset Formula $F\in\Delta(\mathbb{R})$
\end_inset

,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
c(F,u(w_{1}+\cdot))\leq c(F,u(w_{2}+\cdot))
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $c(\cdot)$
\end_inset

 is the minimum final wealth the agent is willing to accept.
 Equivalently,
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
c(w_{1}+F,u)-w_{1} & \leq & c(w_{2}+F,u)-w_{2}\\
w_{2}-c(w_{2},+F,u) & \leq & w_{1}-c(w_{1}+F,u)
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $w-c(\cdot)$
\end_inset

 represents the maximum amount the agent is willing to pay to get rid of
 risk.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
For each 
\begin_inset Formula $w_{2}>w_{1}$
\end_inset

, each 
\begin_inset Formula $t\in\mathbb{R}$
\end_inset

, and each 
\begin_inset Formula $\epsilon>0$
\end_inset

,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\pi(t,\epsilon,u(w_{1}+\cdot))\geq\pi(t,\epsilon,u(w_{2}+\cdot))
\]

\end_inset

Equivalently, for each 
\begin_inset Formula $x>y$
\end_inset

,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\pi(y,\epsilon,u)\geq\pi(x,\epsilon,u)
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Proof
Will prove.
 Check Mas-Colell page 193.
 The results in Theorem 25 follow directly from Theorem 24.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Here, we will prove that 
\begin_inset Formula $c(F,u(w+\cdot))=c(w+F)-w$
\end_inset

.
 By the definition of the certainty equivalent, 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\ v:\mathbb{R}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}:v(c(F,u))=\int vdF\equiv E\{v(F)\}$
\end_inset

.
 When 
\begin_inset Formula $v=u(w+\cdot)$
\end_inset

, then
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
u(w+c(F,u(w+\cdot))) & = & E\{u(w+F)\}\\
u(w+c(F,u(w+\cdot))) & = & u(c(w+F,u))
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

Since 
\begin_inset Formula $u$
\end_inset

 is monotone, then we have
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
w+c(F,u(w+\cdot)) & = & c(w+F,u)\\
c(F,u(w+\cdot)) & = & c(w+F,u)-w
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

