\begin_layout Proof
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $l=\alpha\delta_{x}+(1-\alpha)\delta_{y}$
\end_inset

 where 
\begin_inset Formula $0<x<y$
\end_inset

.
 Consider an expected utility maximizer 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

 with initial wealth 
\begin_inset Formula $w$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof

\bar under
Part A.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

If the agent owns the lottery already, then the minimum price 
\begin_inset Formula $\overline{p}$
\end_inset

 at which she is willing to sell satisfies
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
u(w+\overline{p}) & = & E\left\{ u(w+l)\right\} =\alpha u(w+x)+(1-\alpha)u(w+y)\\
 & = & u\left[c(l,u(w+\cdot))\right]
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $c(l,u(w+\cdot))$
\end_inset

 is the certainty equivalent of 
\begin_inset Formula $l$
\end_inset

 with respect to 
\begin_inset Formula $u(w+\cdot)$
\end_inset

.
 Hence,
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
w+\overline{p} & = & c(l,u(w+\cdot))\\
\overline{p} & = & c(l,u(w+\cdot))-w
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

Since 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu_{l}=\alpha x+(1-\alpha)y$
\end_inset

, then 
\begin_inset Formula $w+x\leq c(l,u(w+\cdot))\leq w+\mu_{l}$
\end_inset

, and thus, 
\begin_inset Formula $c(l,u(w+\cdot))\geq w$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof

\bar under
Part B.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

If the agent does not currently own the lottery, the maximum price 
\begin_inset Formula $\underline{p}$
\end_inset

 at which she is willing to buy satisfies
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
u(w) & = & E\left\{ u(w-\underline{p}+l)\right\} =\alpha u(w-\underline{p}+x)+(1-\alpha)u(w-\underline{p}+y)\\
 & = & u\left[c(l,u([w-\underline{p}]+\cdot])\right]
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

where 
